---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import HayateRig from "../components/HayateRig.astro";
import { url } from "../lib";
---

<Base title="About" description="What Hayate is and how the project works.">
  <article>
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      Give Hayate a web page and she tells you what it's about: a marathon plan becomes
      <strong>Sports › Running</strong>, a Lisbon hotel review becomes <strong>Travel › Hotels</strong>.
      The catch is that she has to do it in <strong>under a millisecond</strong>, on a plain CPU, for almost no money.
    </p>
    <img src={url("pipeline.svg")} alt="Pipeline: public pages, a big LLM labels them, tiny models learn to imitate it, then accuracy, speed and cost are measured" />
    <p>
      A big LLM (the <em>teacher</em>) labels public web pages once, using the IAB content categories. Small
      models (the <em>students</em>) learn to copy those labels, from hashed word patterns to a compact transformer.
      Each is measured for quality, latency and cost per million pages. The result is a trade-off curve, not a
      single model.
    </p>
    <p>
      It's a personal weekend project that uses only public data and public models. It categorizes pages,
      not people.
    </p>
    <h2>Meet Hayate-chan</h2>
    <HayateRig />
    <p>
      Hayate-chan is the model itself, drawn as a character (疾風, "gale"): smug, fast and slightly impatient with
      anything slow. When she gets a page right, that's the model; when she trips over a cookie wall, that's the model
      too. Her look is fixed in a <a href="https://github.com/HenriqueOkuti/hayate/blob/main/docs/mascot.md">canon</a>,
      so she stays the same from post to post.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
